  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nCurrently, strong S/WV trough moving southeast across the \nNorthern Plains today, with an associated sfc cold front now to\nour south. Behind the frontal passage earlier, strong northwest\nwinds have developed along with the extensive low level stratus\nfiltering into the state. Peak gusts have been right around 50 \nmph, and should continue to be gusty for several more hours till\ndiminishing this evening/early Friday morning. Thus wind \nheadlines remain on track. With the mid level wave, \nisolated/scattered light rain showers moving south/southeast, so\nwill maintain this in the forecast. Best moisture accumulation\nwill be over my east, where a few hundreths will be possible.\n\nS/WV troughing to our east tonight with a high amplitude ridge \nto our west. Strong surface high pressure builds into the \nwestern Dakotas later tonight, thus the decrease in winds \nforecast. In addition, CAA coupled with decreasing clouds and \nthe decrease in winds will see temperatures dropping into the \n20s west and north central, and to near freezing south central.\nShould remain above freezing southeast where winds and clouds \nlinger the longest. Thus, we opted to issue a Freeze Warning for\nwestern and much of central North Dakota late tonight through \nearly Friday. \n\nSouth/southwest return flow for Friday, yet the cold start will\nonly see highs in the mid 40s to mid 50s despite a mostly clear\nsky as well. Fire weather may become an issue southwest Friday\nand Saturday, but right now looks sub-marginal. \n\nUpper level ridge to our west deamplifies into the weekend as \nit moves east, morphing into a quasi-zonal flow regime across \nthe Northern Plains into next week. This pattern will bring dry \nweather conditions to the region, along with temperatures at or \nslightly above normal.",
